keith@majestix.cs.uoregon.edu (Keith Steiger) writes:

>Now on to my personal gripes:
>
>Monsters have much more knowledge than they should.  For example, there was a
>level in the Dungeon of Doom with a polymorph trap on it.  I didn't think it
>too horrible, especially since I had no cat... at that point, anyway.  However,
>every monster that enters this room IMMEDIATELY dives for the polymorph trap.
>In the last case, the elf could not have seen anyone else enter the trap, and
>I was the only creature in the room capable of speech.  He polymorphed into
>a demi-lich, and my game was over.
>
>Furthermore, the monsters have total knowledge of every wand, scroll and potion
>that comes into their possession.  If I drop a wand of fire with 1 charge,
>that charge will be fired at me.  However, if I drop a wand of speed monster
>with 1 charge, they will zap themselves with it.  How could they know?  This
>bothers me no end.


To ameliorate the new ability of monsters to carry and use weapons, and wands
and other magic items, with no forewarning, wouldn't it make sense to add
the capability for a player to see what items a monster is carrying?  At
least items that would be obvious, such as external armor, weapon(s) wielded,
and tools in use (though this capability hasn't been added yet).  Regular
pack items that are not carried in containers like bags or chests would
be a trickier matter.  One would want dangerous items like wands to be
seen, the prompting for this feature in the first place, but it doesn't
necessarily follow that little gemstones in a monster's pack (do monsters
even have packs?) also be visible.  Perhaps a notion of "wieldedness" should
be required for all items, for consistency, and only wielded items be
viewable.  How ARE we zapping wands when our hands are full with a big
sword and a shield, anyway?

Anyway, the ability should work in reverse, too -- monsters should be
able to glance us over and see what we've got, and make intelligent
decisions based on that information, just as we would with them.
This would make the game even more exciting and more of a mental
exercise.

I think that monsters already have everything pre-identified because
there isn't an obviously good way to work it otherwise.  Unlike
us, the monsters have short and futureless lives...if they were
required to identify everything before using it, they wouldn't get
*far* while they ran around for a scroll of identify to see what
that wand is they've got.  A large list of "identified" info would
have to be maintained for each individual monster, eating memory
madly.  And there is some rationalization for this -- it is their
dungeon after all.  But I agree that a relatively dimwitted gnome
should not know all magic items by birthright, a feat which takes
a human explorer long suffering to learn.

-jim


